ch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Lamarck\uc1 \u8217 ?s style is generally very clear and straightforward, but it is also very loose and repetitive. It lacks much of the variety and grace which, for example, one finds in the scientific writing of his colleague Cuvier. One sometimes irritating feature of Lamarck\uc1 \u8217 ?s style is his tendency to use many words where two or three will convey the meaning just as well. In dealing with this feature, I have tried to render Lamarck\uc1 \u8217 ?s meaning precisely in a style which comes as close as possible to modern English, even if that means, at times, altering Lamarck\uc1 \u8217 ?s original phrasing (so, for example, rather than saying \uc1 \u8220 ?They launch themselves into the bosom of the air\uc1 \u8221 ? this translation states \uc1 \u8220 ?They fly in the air\uc1 \u8221 ?). However, it has not been possible to avoid the repetitiveness in many places.}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af6 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \keep0 \keepn0 \aspalpha \aspnum \toplinepunct0 \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af6 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \keep0 \keepn0 \aspalpha \aspnum \toplinepunct0 \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af6 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang